{"seed_id": "BigCodeBench/363", "verdict": "update", "note": "Prompt says to print the pair using the input token's number, but for input \"007\" the gold output prints \"7\" (canonical int, no leading zeros) — prompt doesn't state this normalization.", "author": "author_2", "prompt": "Standard input contains zero or more whitespace-separated tokens on a single line, each token meant to represent a number for which you must compute the factorial.\n\nFirst validate the input: every token must be a string that represents a non-negative integer (only digits, optionally preceded by nothing else — no minus sign, no decimal point, no letters). If any token fails this check (for example it is negative, non-numeric, or a decimal), print exactly:\n\nValueError\n\nand nothing else, then stop.\n\n\nIf all tokens are valid non-negative integers, convert each token to its integer value and compute its factorial. Then print the results as one line containing, for each distinct integer value (keeping the order in which it first appears in the input), the pair \"number:factorial\" separated by a single space between pairs, where \"number\" is the canonical decimal form of the integer (i.e. without any leading zeros — for example the token \"003\" is printed as \"3\"). If the input line is empty (no tokens), print an empty line.\n\n\nExample:\nInput:\n5 6 7\n\nOutput:\n5:120 6:720 7:5040"}
